\subsection{Depth test}
\label{sec:v3-depthtest}

\begin{definition}[hub null and excess B]
\label{def:hubnull}
Given a frame that assigns each centroid $x_i$ to a cluster $k(i)$ with hub $h_{k(i)}$, the mean of its cluster, write $x_i=h_{k(i)}+o_i$. The hub null keeps every offset $o_i$ and replaces the hub configuration by a Haar resample with the hubs' spectrum, $x'_i=h'_{k(i)}+o_i$; the excess of Eq.~\ref{eq:excess} read against this null is $e_B$, and it measures hierarchy above the clusters of the frame only.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}[matched star]
\label{def:star}
The matched star of a cloud keeps the real clusters, each as a Haar sample with the cluster's own covariance, and places their hubs as Gaussian draws at the real hub radius, so that it carries the clustering of the real cloud and no hierarchy above it. Its spectrum-matched-hub variant draws the hubs as a Haar resample of the real hubs instead.
\end{definition}
With $e_B$ read on the real cloud and on its matched star, the depth statistic and its standardized form are
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{depth}=e_B(X)-e_B(X_{\mathrm{star}}),\qquad z=\mathrm{depth}\,/\,\mathrm{s.d.},
\label{eq:depth}
\end{equation}
where the spread is taken over star seeds and null replicates, and a cloud is certified as more hierarchical than a star when $z\le-2$. The test is needed because of the star caveat: a pure star of clusters with no hierarchy already passes the census of Definition~\ref{def:genuine}, so the excess measures how classes are arranged and not whether the arrangement has depth. The frame is the WordNet cut on ImageNet and the coarse labels on CIFAR-100. The false-alarm rate and the power of the test are measured on real clouds in Section~\ref{sec:v3-depth}.

\subsection{Comparing trees across models}
\label{sec:v3-treemap}

Each model's hierarchy is a dendrogram over the shared class centroids, and two hierarchies are compared by the adjusted Rand index between their cut partitions, by the correlation between their cophenetic distances, and by their agreement on which pair of a random class triplet merges first, a measure that needs no cut. A configuration of metric and linkage is selected by a criterion fixed without reference to the agreements being compared: the highest cophenetic fidelity among configurations whose reference cuts are non-degenerate for every model, applied per dataset because degeneracy is a property of the dataset. Local agreement is read with the permutation calibration of \citet{groger2026aristotelian}: permutations of the class correspondence give the null distribution of mutual-kNN agreement and of centroid CKA, the upper quantile of that distribution gives the threshold, and the calibrated score is the excess over the threshold rescaled to keep the maximum at one.

\subsection{Projection and tasks}
\label{sec:v3-projection}

A parameter-free map centers the features, scales the 95th-percentile norm to radius $1/\sqrt2$ and preserves directions, so that Euclidean, cosine and Poincar\'{e} readouts can be compared on the intact representation. Nearest-centroid classification and 5-way 5-shot episodes score the three readouts on every cell of the task grid. The next section fixes the models, the data and the protocol on which the instrument is run. % exp1, exp2

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:v3-setup}

\paragraph{Models.} Twelve vision backbones form the census: four supervised ViTs trained on ImageNet-21k, ViT-T to ViT-L, DINO-B, four DINOv2 sizes from S to G, CLIP-B, CLIP-L and SigLIP-B. Two self-supervised ResNet-50 backbones read the same census in the appendix. Two ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k leaf labels, DeiT-B and the augreg ViT-B, serve as the label-set control. MERU and its Euclidean CLIP twin, in three sizes, serve as the hyperbolic-backbone control. Sixteen text models read the thousand ImageNet class names: four GPT-2 sizes, three Pythia sizes, two OLMo sizes and seven sentence embedders. Parameter counts and the full panel are in Table~\ref{tab:q14-panel}.


\paragraph{Datasets and cells.} The vision census reads six class sets: ImageNet and CIFAR-100, which carry a real hierarchy, CIFAR-10 and DTD, and the flat FMNIST and MNIST. Text is read on the ImageNet class names, on DBpedia Classes, a three-level ontology independent of WordNet, and on HierarCaps, a four-level caption hierarchy. A cell is one pair of model and class set. The premise is read at the sample level, on per-image features, roughly a thousand stratified training images per cell for CIFAR-100 and DTD; hierarchy is read at the class level, on class centroids.

\paragraph{Extraction.} Vision backbones give the encoder's default pooled or CLS embedding, and class centroids average a hundred cached training images per class on ImageNet and the full cached split on the other datasets. Causal language models give the last-token hidden state of ``a photo of a \{class\}'', extracted one prompt at a time, because left-padded batching alters GPT-2's hidden states; embedders use their recommended pooling, and the CLIP and SigLIP text towers their text encoders (Appendix~\ref{app:textcensus}).

\paragraph{Replicates, seeds, budgets and frames.} Every cell is read against 200 Haar replicates, with half a million sampled quadruples per seed and ten seeds for the real cloud, and genuineness is decided by Benjamini--Hochberg over the 72 vision cells and, separately, over the text census. The depth test uses the WordNet cut into thirty superclasses on ImageNet and the twenty coarse labels on CIFAR-100, a pre-specified balanced frame as the control, a sweep over the number of clusters, and ten star seeds under the hub null. Its false-alarm rate and power are measured on synthetic hierarchies at ImageNet's class count and on the real ImageNet clouds with implanted two-level trees. With the setup fixed, we read the premise where it is read.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:form}

\subsection{The premise at the sample level}
\label{sec:v3-sample}

\paragraph{The premise does not survive calibration.} On per-image features the raw reading lies in the low band the literature reports. Calibrated against the matched null, it is not genuine in {{SL_WITHIN}} of 24 cells. Where it is genuine, the excess removes at most {{SL_FRAC_HI}} per cent of the null reading, and the largest figure belongs to DINOv2-G on CIFAR-100 images. The genuine cells belong mostly to the DINOv2 family, whose structure is the angular tree of Section~\ref{sec:content}, which cosine collects. Table~\ref{tab:q3-sample} gives the reading per cell, with the raw supremum the literature reports beside it. % expR62_samplelevel_record.csv

\subsection{Clustered structure at the class level}
\label{sec:v3-class}

