\input{tab_census}

\paragraph{Almost every cell is below its null.} On class centroids the excess is negative in {{NEG_COUNT}} of 72 cells. It is genuine in 49 of 72. Table~\ref{tab:census} gives the census of record row by row, and Figure~\ref{fig:excess} shows which markers fall outside the null band. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{The count survives resampling.} On ImageNet and CIFAR-100, the two sets with a real hierarchy, the count is 18 of 24. Folding image resampling and estimator noise into the null, or repeating the census on thirty resampled centroid sets, leaves between {{R11_LO}} and {{R11_HI}} of the 72 cells genuine, and the cells that move are on the transfer sets (Table~\ref{tab:q8-robust}). % expR66_joint_sensitivity_summary.csv

\paragraph{The structure belongs to no single family.} Every family contributes genuine cells. The smallest backbone, ViT-T, is genuine on DTD only. The flat datasets sit near the resolution limit of ten classes, and a class-count control on ImageNet subsets shows that the reading follows the hierarchy of the labels rather than their number, as Table~\ref{tab:q8-robust} reports. Two self-supervised ResNet-50 backbones read the same clustered structure as the ViTs (Table~\ref{tab:q1-census}). % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR60_c_sweep_record.csv, expR45_convnet_rows.csv

\paragraph{The excess is small in absolute terms and large against the null.} On ImageNet centroids the excess lies between {{NULLU_LO}} and {{NULLU_HI}} null spreads below the null mean. The absolute values are in Table~\ref{tab:census}. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{The star caveat.} A synthetic star of thirty clusters with no hierarchy scores an excess as deep as any real cell, reaching ${{STAR_DEEP}}$ under the Haar null. A negative excess therefore says clustered, not deep, and the depth of Section~\ref{sec:v3-depth} needs the test of Definition~\ref{def:star} (Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration}). % expR42_star_calibration.csv

\paragraph{Neural collapse is the limit, not the reading.} Neural collapse predicts that the class means of a supervised classifier converge to a simplex equiangular tight frame \citep{papyan2020prevalence}, and an exact frame is the extreme case of the star caveat. The arrangement we certify is not that frame: class means cluster around superclass hubs that a frame would erase, cutting the centroid dendrograms recovers the CIFAR-100 superclasses, and the same clustered structure appears without class labels and in causal language models, where neural collapse is not stated (Table~\ref{tab:q11-interventions}). Scale deepens the excess for one family only: the DINOv2 rows of Figure~\ref{fig:excess} reach further left with size on CIFAR-10 and, weakly, on CIFAR-100. No other family shows a trend with scale, and training moves the raw reading within an architecture: fine-tuning on a task without class hierarchy raises it, and it falls across transformer depth, as Table~\ref{tab:q11-interventions} reports. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, analysis4_finetuning.csv, e1_delta_by_layer.csv

\subsection{Depth above the labelled clusters}
\label{sec:v3-depth}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_depth_test.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The depth test certifies hierarchy above the superclasses in {{N_IN}} of 12 ImageNet backbones, never fires on randomized hubs, and misses implanted depth at ImageNet's noise level.} (a) Depth $z$ per backbone under the matched star; filled markers are certified ($z\le-2$). (b) Detection rate against implant strength on the real clouds: zero at $s=0$ and low at $s=1$ with the real within-cluster spread, full once that spread is shrunk into the validated range (dashed). % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv
}
\label{fig:depth}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Depth is certified in four of twelve.} On ImageNet the real cloud is more hierarchical than its matched star in {{N_IN}} of 12 backbones, ViT-S, ViT-B, ViT-L and DINOv2-L, and never less. Under the spectrum-matched-hub star the certified set is the same four, with $z$ from ${{HAAR_Z_HI}}$ to ${{HAAR_Z_LO}}$. Figure~\ref{fig:depth}a shows the four filled markers, and Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} gives both stars per backbone. % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR69_depth_haarhubs_summary.csv

\paragraph{The test raises no false alarm on real clouds.} With the hub arrangement of every real cloud randomized, the test fires in {{FA_HAAR}} runs. For the four certified backbones the same clusters with random hubs never fire, {{CERT_S0}}, so their verdict comes from the real hub arrangement. % expR69_depth_haarhubs_summary.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv

\paragraph{The test is weak at ImageNet's noise level.} A clean implanted two-level tree is detected in almost none of the runs, a power of {{POWER_S1}}. The reason is the noise level: the within-cluster spread of the real clouds is {{RATIO_LO}} to {{RATIO_HI}} times their between-hub spread, outside the range the synthetic sweep covered. Figure~\ref{fig:depth}b shows the detection rate staying at zero below full strength with the real spread, and Table~\ref{tab:q5-power} gives the power by strength. The eight other backbones are not detected, which is not the same as not hierarchical. The certified set shifts with the choice of frame. CIFAR-100, with a hundred centroids in twenty clusters, lies outside the validated regime, and its readings are reported without certification. An earlier isotropic star produced false alarms and is withdrawn. A variant with the within-cluster offsets shrunk into the validated range detects all twelve backbones, but it also fires at zero strength for two contrastive backbones, so it is a diagnostic of power and not a substitute test. % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv, expR64b_wn30_summary.csv, expR64b_wn30bal_summary.csv

\paragraph{Leaf-label supervision can produce the depth.} The augreg ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k leaf labels is certified, at $z={{AUG_Z}}$ under the Gaussian-hub star and under the spectrum-matched one alike. It is also aligned with WordNet, at a Spearman correlation of ${{AUG_RHO}}$, like the ImageNet-21k ViTs. % expR70_inet1k_supervised.csv

\paragraph{But it does not guarantee it.} DeiT-B, trained on the same leaf labels with another recipe, is not certified and is nearly unaligned, at ${{DEIT_RHO}}$. It nevertheless recovers the CIFAR-100 superclasses from its dendrograms at an adjusted Rand index of {{DEIT_ARI}}. Hierarchical label sets are therefore not the explanation of the certified ViTs' depth, and alignment with the human taxonomy is recipe-dependent even among leaf-supervised ViTs (Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth}). % expR70_inet1k_supervised.csv

\paragraph{Imposing the geometry does not create detected depth.} MERU embeds images and text on the Lorentz hyperboloid with an entailment objective, and its released Euclidean twin shares backbone, data and recipe minus the hyperbolic lift \citep{desai2023meru}. The two read the same excess on ImageNet images, MERU's reading in its own Lorentz metric equals its Euclidean reading to the fourth decimal, and neither is detected as more hierarchical than a matched star, with MERU never ahead of its twin. Its embeddings never leave the near-flat regime of the hyperboloid: the spatial norm times the square root of the curvature stays between {{MERU_RAD_LO}} and {{MERU_RAD_HI}} at the median. Lorentz and Euclidean pairwise distances agree at a ratio of ${{MERU_RATIO}}$, so the control tests the training objective rather than a curved geometry (Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth}). % expR63_meru_record.csv, expR71_meru_radii.csv

\subsection{Whose tree}
\label{sec:content}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures/fig_treemap_controls.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The categorical island is a chaining artifact; about a third of the within-block agreement is missing under most configurations.} Pairwise ARI between the dendrogram cuts of the 12 vision models on ImageNet under the naive configuration (a) and the selected one (b), with the mean DINOv2-vs-block ARI above each panel. % exp22, exp23
}
\label{fig:treemap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{The naive map manufactures an island.} Under Euclidean distances with average linkage the DINOv2 family agrees with the supervised and contrastive block at an adjusted Rand index of {{NAIVE_BIG}} against {{NAIVE_BLOCK}} within the block. The island is a chaining artifact: DINOv2's Euclidean dendrograms put almost every class in one cluster, so the index is forced to zero by construction, and DINO-B does not fall in the island. Figure~\ref{fig:treemap}a shows the naive map. % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, exp23_config_diagnostics.json

\paragraph{Controlling the cut leaves a moderate gap.} Under the criterion-selected configuration, cosine distances with average linkage, the DINOv2 family agrees with the block at {{ARI_BIG}} against {{ARI_BLOCK}} within the block (Figure~\ref{fig:treemap}b). % exp23_treemap_controls.npz

\paragraph{The gap holds under every admissible cut.} Under the other three admissible configurations the DINOv2 family agrees with the block at {{ADM_LO}} to {{ADM_HI}}. Within the block the agreement is {{ADM_BLK_LO}} to {{ADM_BLK_HI}}, so about a third of it is missing under most configurations. % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, expR58_treemap_cutfree_summary.csv

\paragraph{The gap depends on the measure.} In cophenetic correlation the gap stays at {{COPH_BIG}} against {{COPH_BLOCK}}. In triplet agreement, which needs no cut, it closes to {{TRIP_BIG}} against {{TRIP_BLOCK}} under cosine-average and persists under every other configuration (Table~\ref{tab:q6-treemap}). % expR58_treemap_cutfree_summary.csv

\paragraph{The self-supervised tree is angular.} DINOv2's semantics concentrate in the angular component of its representation. For DINOv2-L, sibling triplets on CIFAR-100 are resolved at {{TRIP_L_C}} against {{TRIP_L_E}} under cosine and under Euclidean distance respectively. For DINOv2-G the two figures are {{TRIP_G_C}} and {{TRIP_G_E}}. This angular tree is why the Euclidean dendrograms chain into the island and why the triplet gap closes only under cosine-average. % exp10_local_vs_global.csv

\paragraph{The cosine census agrees with the Euclidean one.} Repeating the census on L2-normalized centroids with geodesic distances and the same Haar null, the verdict matches the Euclidean record in {{AGREE}} of 72 cells, and DINOv2 on ImageNet is genuine under both readings. The cosine census is robustness, never the record (Table~\ref{tab:q1-census}). % expR57_census_cosine_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{WordNet alignment follows supervision and recipe.} The Spearman correlation between inter-centroid and WordNet distances on ImageNet is ${{RHO_CON_LO}}$ to ${{RHO_CON_HI}}$ for the contrastive VLMs. It is ${{RHO_SUP_LO}}$ to ${{RHO_SUP_HI}}$ for the supervised ViTs. % exp3_alignment.csv

\paragraph{DINOv2 is the least aligned and the most robust.} For DINOv2 the correlation is ${{RHO_D2_LO}}$ to ${{RHO_D2_HI}}$, with shuffle and Gaussian controls at zero. Supervision deepens the resemblance to the human taxonomy on average across the supervised backbones tested, and the DeiT-B case of Section~\ref{sec:v3-depth} shows that the effect is recipe-dependent. Across the three families, form and content anti-correlate: the most aligned families carry the most fragile excess, and DINOv2, the least aligned, the most robust one (Table~\ref{tab:census}). % exp3_alignment.csv, expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{The recovery is not WordNet circularity.} On DBpedia Classes, an ontology independent of WordNet, the three text embedders read a genuine excess of ${{DBP_EXC_HI}}$ to ${{DBP_EXC_LO}}$ with every replicate above the real value. Their sibling-triplet agreement there is {{DBP_TRIP}}, and no clustering configuration degenerates. % expR61_dbpedia_record.csv, exp14_dbpedia.csv, exp27_dbpedia_treemap.json

\paragraph{The same reading holds on a four-level caption hierarchy.} On HierarCaps the text encoders resolve sibling triplets at {{HC_TRIP_LO}} to {{HC_TRIP_HI}} and order the levels by projection radius, with no class set involved (Table~\ref{tab:q7-wordnet}). % exp16_hierarcaps.csv

\paragraph{The text census depends on recipe, scale and probe.} On the thousand ImageNet class names, {{TEXT_GEN}} of 15 text models are genuine under the record. GPT-2 S is genuine at $p={{GPT2S_P}}$ and flips under the supremum and under cosine, GPT-2 M is not genuine, and GPT-2 L and XL are genuine under every reading. Pythia is genuine at every scale tested, and so is OLMo-1B. The sentence embedders are absent on the class names and present on DBpedia, so the probe decides their verdict. Prompt template and extraction protocol move the fragile values but not the scale ordering (Table~\ref{tab:q2-text}). % expR53_text_haar_p999_200.csv, expR49_template_nulls_bs1.csv, expR47_extraction_variance.csv

\subsection{What is shared is local and ordinal}
\label{sec:v3-local}

\paragraph{Local agreement survives calibration.} Permutation-calibrated mutual-kNN agreement over the sixty-six model pairs is {{KNN_CAL}}, and every pair is significant at the resolution of the permutation null. Calibrated centroid CKA is {{CKA_CAL}}, and the Poincar\'{e} readout improves neither reading. With the cut-free measures of Section~\ref{sec:content}, this is the reading of \citet{groger2026aristotelian} on geometry: shared neighborhoods, a model-specific metric, and what a practitioner can collect from them is the subject of the next section (Table~\ref{tab:q13-local}). % exp21b_local_global_K200.csv

\section{Implications for Hyperbolic Representation Learning}
\label{sec:practice}

\paragraph{The raw reading cannot select a curvature.} \citet{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR} set the curvature of the Poincar\'{e} ball from the measured relative hyperbolicity,
\begin{equation}
c=\big(0.144/\delta_{\text{rel}}\big)^{2},\qquad \delta_{\text{rel}}=2\delta/\mathrm{diam}.
\label{eq:curvature}
\end{equation}
Evaluated on the Gaussian band of Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration}, Eq.~\ref{eq:curvature} assigns structureless clouds a curvature from ${{C_LO}}$ at $d{=}{{D_LO}}$ to ${{C_HI}}$ at $d{=}{{D_HI}}$, so the rule tracks the backbone's width rather than its hierarchy. % exp1_delta_controls.csv

\paragraph{The calibrated reading predicts the zero-cost gain.} Within each hierarchical dataset, the record excess predicts the nearest-centroid gain of the Poincar\'{e} readout on all four datasets and the few-shot gain on three, with confidence intervals that exclude zero and a family control that leaves the within-dataset relation in place. The depth verdict predicts no gain anywhere (Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary}). % expR68_corollary_excess.csv

\paragraph{A zero-cost readout collects what is there.} Cosine collects the self-supervised structure. The Poincar\'{e} readout adds from ${{GAIN_LO}}$ to ${{GAIN_HI}}$ pp over cosine for the contrastive VLMs on prototype tasks, and its effect on the other families is inconsistent in sign. Nothing helps on flat labels, and sample-level tasks never benefit. Before imposing curvature, then, a practitioner should read the excess and its rank, the depth test, whose positive verdict certifies and whose negative verdict says nothing, and the cosine reading that says where the structure lives (Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary}). % exp2b_normalized_stack.csv, exp2_metric_controls.csv, exp24_val_metric_selection.csv

\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Conclusion.} Read correctly, foundation models organize classes into clustered structure that is occasionally hierarchical and moderately shared; they do not converge to one common tree, and their raw tree-likeness is not evidence for hyperbolic geometry. The premise of latent hyperbolicity is confounded, and its sample-level reading sits within null noise. What survives at the class level is clustered structure in most cells, hierarchy above the superclasses in a few backbones, no detected depth from imposing the geometry, and a moderately shared, supervision-dependent recovery of the human taxonomy, angular for the self-supervised family. Curvature should be measured rather than assumed, and a zero-cost readout collects what is there.

\paragraph{Limitations.} (i)~The excess certifies structure beyond second moments, star-like clustering included, and depth needs the separate test. (ii)~The depth test is conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level, certifies hierarchy above its frame only, leaves hierarchy within the clusters untested, and has no trained positive control that settles it, as Table~\ref{tab:q5-power} shows. (iii)~The census is class-centroid geometry on six datasets. (iv)~Causal-LM values, not their scale ordering, are template-conditioned. (v)~The objective--geometry link is correlational, resting chiefly on DINOv2's scale range and on two leaf-label ViTs. (vi)~Downstream scope is prototype-based metric tasks with modest gains. (vii)~The descriptor $\xi$ tracks norm dispersion rather than curvature (Table~\ref{tab:q12-xi}), and a causal LM's class representation depends on the extraction protocol as much as on the model. (viii)~The sample-level reading covers two datasets and one image budget.

